---
import CodeBlock from "@/components/code-block.astro";
import Docs from "@/layouts/docs.astro";
import { conceptHref } from "@/lib/concepts";
import { docHref } from "@/lib/docs";

const store = `import { createMemoryHistory, FileMode } from "@statewalker/vcs-core";

const history = createMemoryHistory();
await history.initialize();

const blob = await history.blobs.store([new TextEncoder().encode("hello\\n")]);
const tree = await history.trees.store([{ mode: FileMode.REGULAR_FILE, name: "hello.txt", id: blob }]);
const who = { name: "Ada", email: "ada@example.com", timestamp: 1700000000, tzOffset: "+0000" };
const commit = await history.commits.store({ tree, parents: [], author: who, committer: who, message: "Say hello\\n" });`;

const loose = `"blob 6\\0hello\\n"                      the bytes that are hashed and stored
SHA-1 of those bytes = ce013625030ba8dba906f756967f9e9ca394464a   (the id)
zlib(those bytes)    → .git/objects/ce/013625030ba8dba906f756967f9e9ca394464a`;

const layers = `History            blobs · trees · commits · tags · refs   (typed, parsed)
  └─ GitObjects    "<type> <size>\\0<content>", SHA-1 over the whole, zlib
       └─ store    bytes by id (loose files, packs, rows, keys…)`;
---

<Docs title="The object model" description="Git's four object types, the loose object format, why an object's id is the SHA-1 of its bytes, and how packs hold many objects with deltas, as StateWalker VCS implements them.">
  <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground"><a href="/docs/concepts">Concepts</a> · run it: <a href="/examples/object-model">Object model example</a></p>
  <h1>The object model</h1>
  <p>
    A repository's history is a set of immutable objects, each named by a hash of its bytes.
    <code>vcs-core</code> stores and reads them exactly as git does, so the ids it computes are the
    ids git computes and the bytes it writes are bytes git can read. The
    <a href="/examples/object-model">object model example</a> stores a blob, a tree and a commit in
    your tab and checks each id against the bytes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="objects">Four kinds of object, linked by id</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>blob</strong>: the content of one file, nothing else (no name, no mode).</li>
    <li><strong>tree</strong>: a directory: entries of a mode, a name and an id. The modes are
      <code>FileMode.TREE</code> (a subdirectory), <code>REGULAR_FILE</code>,
      <code>EXECUTABLE_FILE</code>, <code>SYMLINK</code> and <code>GITLINK</code> (a submodule's commit).</li>
    <li><strong>commit</strong>: a tree id, the parent commit ids (none for the first commit, two or
      more for a merge), author, committer and a message.</li>
    <li><strong>tag</strong>: an annotated tag: the id and type of the object it names, a tag name,
      a tagger and a message.</li>
  </ul>
  <CodeBlock code={store} lang="ts" title="a blob, a tree, a commit" />
  <p>
    Because a commit names its tree and its parents by id, the id of the newest commit fixes the
    whole history below it: change one byte of an old file and every id above it changes.
    <code>history.blobs</code>, <code>trees</code>, <code>commits</code> and <code>tags</code> are
    the typed stores; <code>load</code> returns <code>undefined</code> for a missing id, and for an
    id that names an object of another type
    (<a href={docHref("packages/vcs-core/README.md")}>vcs-core reference</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="loose-format">An object is "type size\0content", compressed with zlib</h2>
  <p>
    Every object is stored as one byte string: the type, a space, the content's length in decimal,
    a NUL byte, then the content. That string, compressed with zlib, is a git loose object, the
    file under <code>.git/objects/&lt;2 hex&gt;/&lt;38 hex&gt;</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={loose} lang="text" title="a loose object" />
  <p>
    All the typed stores go through one kernel, <code>GitObjects</code>, which adds the header,
    computes the id and compresses; it never parses commits or trees, so bytes git produced keep
    their id even when they carry headers the commit parser does not model.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={layers} lang="text" title="where the format lives" />
  <p>
    The size comes first in the hashed bytes, so a write needs it before the content. When a caller
    does not know it (a download of unknown length), the kernel keeps up to 1 MiB in memory and
    spools anything longer to the store's temporary storage, then reads it again. Content always
    flows as <code>AsyncIterable&lt;Uint8Array&gt;</code>, so a large file is never held whole.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ids">An id is the SHA-1 of those bytes</h2>
  <p>
    The id is the SHA-1 of the uncompressed header-and-content string, as 40 lowercase hex digits.
    That is why <code>printf 'hello\n' | git hash-object --stdin</code> prints
    <code>ce013625…</code> and why the example can check its work with nothing but the browser's
    <code>DecompressionStream</code> and Web Crypto: inflate the stored bytes, hash them, compare
    with the id. An object received with its id (<code>putRaw</code>) is refused unless its bytes
    hash to that id.
  </p>
  <p>
    Only SHA-1 is implemented. Asking for SHA-256 (<code>createVcsCore(deps, &#123; hash: "sha256" &#125;)</code>)
    throws <code>sha256 not yet supported</code> instead of silently writing SHA-1 ids.
  </p>

  <h2 id="packs">Packs hold many objects, some as deltas</h2>
  <p>
    Loose objects are one file each. For transfer and for compact storage, git puts many objects in
    one <strong>pack</strong>: a 12-byte header with the object count, the entries, and a SHA-1
    trailer, with an <code>.idx</code> beside it to find an entry by id. An entry is a whole object
    or a <strong>delta</strong> against another object: an OFS delta names its base by its offset in
    the same pack, a REF delta by id.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>listObjects</code> + <code>writePack</code> are git's <code>rev-list --objects</code> piped
      into <code>pack-objects</code>: what the receiver lacks, streamed as a pack, reusing stored
      entries and, optionally, searching for deltas in git's window.</li>
    <li><code>importPack</code> keeps a received pack of 100 objects or more as a pack (git's
      <code>transfer.unpackLimit</code>) and unpacks smaller ones into loose objects; stores without
      packs (memory, KV, SQL) unpack everything.</li>
    <li><code>installPack</code> is <code>git index-pack --stdin --fix-thin</code>: it completes a thin
      pack from the repository's objects and writes an <code>.idx</code> byte-identical to git's.</li>
    <li><code>openPackSet</code> reads a pack directory, validating every <code>.idx</code> and
      skipping a corrupt pack instead of failing the repository.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Packs are how objects travel over <a href={conceptHref("transport", "upload-pack")}>the transport</a>,
    and the <code>.git</code> backend reads them before loose objects
    (<a href={conceptHref("storage", "git-layout")}>storage</a>). Not supported:
    <code>multi-pack-index</code>, <code>.rev</code> and bitmap files.
  </p>

